---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';

const title = 'Remote-team scheduling checklist across time zones';
const description =
  'A practical checklist for setting up fair, resilient recurring meetings for remote teams in different time zones.';
---

<Base title={title} description={description} path="/guides/remote-team-scheduling-checklist">
  <article class="max-w-2xl">
    <nav aria-label="Breadcrumb" class="mb-5 text-xs text-paper-500">
      <a href="/guides" class="ring-focus hover:text-paper-900">Guides</a>
      <span aria-hidden="true" class="mx-1.5">/</span>
      <span aria-current="page">Scheduling checklist</span>
    </nav>
    <header class="mb-8">
      <p class="text-[11px] font-semibold tracking-[0.15em] text-paper-500 uppercase">
        Remote teams
      </p>
      <h1 class="mt-2 text-3xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-balance sm:text-4xl">
        A remote-team scheduling checklist
      </h1>
      <p class="mt-4 text-lg leading-relaxed text-paper-700">
        A recurring meeting becomes much easier to maintain when the team agrees on a few details
        before the first invitation goes out. Use this list for a kickoff, handover or standing
        call.
      </p>
    </header>

    <section class="mt-8">
      <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight">Before you choose a time</h2>
      <ul class="mt-3 list-disc space-y-2 pl-5 text-sm leading-relaxed text-paper-700">
        <li>Collect each participant&apos;s city, not just an offset or abbreviation.</li>
        <li>Ask for normal working hours and any non-negotiable boundaries.</li>
        <li>
          Decide whether attendance is essential for everyone or whether asynchronous updates can
          replace some calls.
        </li>
        <li>Choose the city whose local time will anchor the calendar series.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-8">
      <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight">Before you send the invitation</h2>
      <ul class="mt-3 list-disc space-y-2 pl-5 text-sm leading-relaxed text-paper-700">
        <li>Check the selected hour in a tool that uses the actual meeting date.</li>
        <li>Look ahead at the next daylight-saving transition for every location.</li>
        <li>Write the anchor city and the other local times in the event description.</li>
        <li>State whether the time stays fixed in the anchor city or rotates between regions.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-8">
      <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight">For the recurring series</h2>
      <ul class="mt-3 list-disc space-y-2 pl-5 text-sm leading-relaxed text-paper-700">
        <li>Add a reminder before each transition window where the local time changes.</li>
        <li>
          Review the arrangement after a few meetings instead of treating the first compromise as
          permanent.
        </li>
        <li>Rotate inconvenient slots when the team spans a large east–west distance.</li>
        <li>
          Record decisions asynchronously so people outside the overlap are not excluded from the
          work.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-8">
      <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight">Make it shareable</h2>
      <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-paper-700">
        The simplest test is whether every participant can open the same plan and understand why the
        time was chosen. Set up the cities and working hours in the <a
          href="/overlap"
          class="ring-focus rounded font-medium text-paper-900 underline underline-offset-2 hover:text-paper-700"
          >meeting planner</a
        >, then share its link alongside the calendar invitation. It gives the team a common
        reference when daylight saving changes the arithmetic later.
      </p>
    </section>
  </article>
</Base>
